{{Quote|When the storm is over, you can see my racer. I'm building a podracer!|[[Anakin Skywalker]]|<ref name="Episode I">{{Film|I}}</ref>}}
'''Podracers''' were [[repulsorcraft]] used as racing vehicles.

==Description==
Podracers were [[repulsorcraft]] that were built as racing vehicles. Capable of achieving speeds over 700 [[Kilometer per hour|kilometers per hour]], podracers were used in the similarly named sport of [[podracing]].<ref name="Databank" /> The [[P-100 pick-up droid]] was designed to retrieve valuable components from podracers that had crashed and then bring these pieces to the storage hold of a [[P-100 Salvage Droid]].<ref name="CL">[[Star Wars: Complete Locations (2016)|''Star Wars: Complete Locations'']]</ref> Podracers came in various shapes and sizes, although the general construction of a podracer was a one-man [[cockpit]] towed by two [[engine]]s which were attached to the cockpit via two cables.<ref name="Episode I" />

==History==
[[File:AnakinsRadonUlzerPodracer-CGSWG.png|thumb|right|300px|Anakin Skywalker's podracer]]
Podracers piloted by professional [[Podracer (profession)|podracers]], typically diminutive beings with fast reflexes, competed in the [[Boonta Eve Classic]], a high-stakes three-round competition along the winding, dangerous [[Mos Espa podrace circuit]] hosted by [[Jabba Desilijic Tiure|Jabba the Hutt]]. The [[Boonta Eve Classic (32 BBY)|Boonta Eve Classic]] of [[32 BBY]] was unusually won by a [[human]] boy&mdash;[[Anakin Skywalker]]&mdash;who was rewarded for his intuition with the guidance of his [[Force-sensitive|strong connection]] to [[the Force]] and thus won his freedom from [[Watto]] to join [[Qui-Gon Jinn]] and the [[Jedi Order]]. [[Ben Quadinaros]]' podracer had four engines connected to the cockpit which malfunctioned at the start of the race. Another, more unusual podracer was employed by [[Neva Kee]], which was one solid unit, with the cockpit placed in front of the engines. Podracers could also be modified at the owner's whim. The crafty [[Dug]] pilot [[Sebulba]], a veteran of the Mos Espa racecourse, had installed an illegal [[flamethrower]] on [[Sebulba's Podracer|his racer]] to sabotage other podracers. Anakin Skywalker himself flew a [[Anakin Skywalker's Podracer|heavily modified podracer]] that he had partially built from the ground up. Other podracers included the ''[[Ord Pedrovia]]'', which was [[pilot]]ed by [[Gasgano]], and the [[Vulptereen 327]], which was piloted by [[Dud Bolt]]. The pilot [[Clegg Holdfast]] flew a red [[9T9-B Wasp|KT9 ''Wasp'']],<ref name="Episode I" /> a podracer which was manufactured by the company [[Keizar-Volvec]].<ref name="EV">''[[Endless Vigil]]''</ref>

By the fall of the [[Galactic Empire]], podracing had lost much popularity and was outlawed by the [[New Republic]].<ref name="Black Spire">''[[Galaxy's Edge: Black Spire]]''</ref> The engines were often redesigned for other uses, such as [[Cobb Vanth]]'s [[speeder]], which was constructed using the engine of a podracer.<ref name="Chapter 9">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 9: The Marshal}}</ref> Some engines were even used to cook meat.<ref name="Chapter 10">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 10: The Passenger}}</ref> Nevertheless, the podracer Sebulba was involved in the [[Galactic League]], an illicit circuit of various types of repulsorcrafts.<ref name="Reveal article">{{SW|url=news/star-wars-galactic-racer-reveal-trailer|text=We're Going Back to Jakku in the Trailer for ''Star Wars: Galactic Racer'' - Exclusive Interview|archivedate=20251212045257}}</ref>

==Behind the scenes==
Sounds recorded for the podracers in [[Star Wars: Episode I The Phantom Menace|''Star Wars'': Episode I ''The Phantom Menace'']] were later used for the speeders on [[Jakku]] in [[Star Wars: Episode VII The Force Awakens|''Star Wars'': Episode VII ''The Force Awakens'']].<ref name="The Force Awakens' sound designer Matthew Wood on 'Star Wars' Easter eggs">[http://www.dailydot.com/geek/star-wars-force-awakens-sound-design-matthew-wood-interview The Force Awakens' sound designer Matthew Wood on 'Star Wars' Easter eggs] ''TheDailyDot'' (2016-02-04). Retrieved on May 30, 2016. "When we did The Phantom Menace, I remember spending months and months recording vehicles for the podrace. And now that sound of all the vehicles we recorded for the podrace, some of those went into the speeder sounds on Jakku."</ref>

